\n- The PPU begins the frame with whatever t was left from the previous frame (set by the split write at the end).\n- The game wants: scanlines 0-31 → scroll_x=0; scanlines 32-239 → scroll_x=camera.\n\nFor that, during the frame, at the end of the status bar region, the game writes $2005 with the camera scroll (setting t), and the PPU applies horizontal bits at the next scanline (dot 257). So the split write is mid-frame, after scanline 31, setting scroll_x=camera.\n\nAnd for the top: the pre-render/start of frame must have scroll_x=0. That's set by the NMI writing $2005 with 0 (i.e., $073F for the NMI? no...). Hmm.\n\nWait, the NMI writes $073F and $0740 to $2005. If $073F holds the *vertical* scroll and $0740 holds... Hmm. Let me reconsider the mapping: \"0x071C screen_scroll_x (level's left edge, mod 256)\". And $073F/$0740 are \"the scroll bytes the NMI writes to PPUSCROLL\". \n\nMaybe $073F = horizontal scroll actually (a copy of camera). In SMB, I recall $0740 is used for the vertical scroll during the frame and $073F for horizontal? Let me check by reading $073F while camera is nonzero.\n\nActually there's a subtlety: SMB writes the *playfield* scroll during NMI too (for the whole frame), and then does the split by writing 0 for the top AFTER the sprite 0 hit? No, the top is drawn first.\n\nLet me think again about the real SMB technique. I recall now: In SMB, the status bar is at the top and the playfield scrolls. The technique:\n1. During vblank NMI, set the scroll to the PLAYFIELD scroll (horizontal camera, vertical 0). This configures the frame.\n2. Wait for the sprite 0 hit. Sprite 0 is positioned at the TOP of the screen (in the status bar area), at y such that its opaque pixels land at scanline ~31. The sprite 0 hit flag ($2002 bit 6) sets when the PPU renders an opaque sprite pixel over an opaque background pixel. \n3. When the hit is detected, the game writes $2005 with 0,0 (resetting the scroll to 0) — this makes the status bar... no wait the status bar is ABOVE.\n\nHmm, order: sprite 0 hit at scanline 31 means scanlines 0-30 already drawn with the playfield scroll → status bar would have scrolled. Wrong direction.\n\nLet me flip: The game sets scroll 0 at vblank (so the top is fixed), then waits for the sprite 0 hit which occurs at scanline ~31, then writes the playfield scroll. That's the correct direction: top drawn with scroll 0 (status bar fixed), and after scanline 31 the scroll becomes the camera. Yes.\n\nSo the NMI writes $2005 with a *fixed* value (the status bar scroll = 0), i.e., the two writes at $815F/$8165 with $073F/$0740. So $073F/$0740 might both be 0 always? Hmm, but the mapping says NMI writes PPUSCROLL from them and $071C is the camera. And there's a separate routine that writes the camera scroll mid-frame.\n\nBut our trace found NO other $2005 writer besides NMI ($815F/$8165) and the VRAM-buffer-end reset ($8EE6/$8EE9). So where's the camera split write?!\n\nUnless the split write is done via **$2006** (PPUADDR) — writing $2006 sets the v register which contains scroll bits. Let me trace $2006 writes too.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider: maybe SMB sets the horizontal scroll for the playfield by writing $2005 with the camera value during NMI (whole frame), and then to fix the status bar it uses a **sprite 0 hit + writing $2005=0 mid-frame at scanline 31**, so that scanlines 0-30 show... no. Argh.\n\nLet's get empirical. I'll get camera nonzero, then read $073F/$0740 while camera nonzero, and trace $2005 and $2006. And use get_tile_at_pixel at y=20 and y=120 to see the actual scroll per region: earlier at camera 0 both were 0. With camera nonzero, we'll see the split: scroll_x at y=20 should be 0 (status bar), at y=120 should be camera.\n\nWait, earlier at (30,120) the result said scroll_source \"scanline\", scroll_scanline 31, scroll_x 0. So the scanline-based scroll source reports the scroll written at scanline 31. That's the mechanism indicator: the tool tracks PPU scroll writes by scanline. At camera 0 both are 0. With camera nonzero, if the split write happens at scanline 31, then (30,120) would show scroll_x=camera with scroll_scanline=31, and (30,20) would show scroll_source=vblank scroll_x=0.\n\nThe earlier result at (30,120): scroll_source \"scanline\", scroll_scanline 31. That strongly suggests the scroll for the playfield is written at scanline 31. But the trace showed no $2005 write there... unless the tool's \"scanline\" scroll source tracks $2006 writes too, or tracks the PPU v register.\n\nHmm wait, it said scroll_scanline 31 even with scroll_x=0. If no write happened, why report scanline 31? Maybe the tool reports the scanline where the scroll register was last written in the frame, and it happened at scanline 31... but our trace only saw $2005 writes from NMI (during vblank = scanline ~241-260) and the buffer-end reset. So the \"scanline 31\" write must be to $2006 or via $2005 at a PC we didn't catch? Our trace caught all accesses to address $2005 for 30 frames — 180 accesses, all covered. Unless the game writes to a *mirror* address like $2009, $200D, ... $3F05? Writes to PPU registers at mirrored addresses are decoded as the same register! The game could use STA $2005 but also could use any address with low 3 bits = 5. Unlikely though.\n\nOR the tool's scanline-scroll tracking follows writes to $2005 AND $2006 (the t register). Let me trace $2006 writes to check.\n\nActually, I recall now that SMB's split is done by writing **$2005 twice** mid-frame. And the \"wait\" is on sprite 0 hit. The mid-frame write should show up in the trace at a PC. We saw 4 PCs only. Two are the NMI pair; two are the buffer-end reset. So no mid-frame write. Therefore the split must use $2006.\n\nHmm, but hold on: the buffer-end reset ($8EE6) writes 0,0 to $2005 — when does that run? It's at the end of WriteVRAMBuffer, called from NMI at $80C3. That's during vblank. And the NMI pair $815F/$8165 runs later in NMI. Both in vblank.\n\nLet me now check