bne 0x0000 due to unresolved; fine.\n\nHmm, but note the branch target shown as \"0x0000\" because the disassembler doesn't resolve relative targets in this output. OK.\n\nAlso important: at $813D there's a wait loop `LDA $2002 / AND #$40 / BNE $813D` — this waits until sprite 0 flag is CLEAR, but only if $0722 != 0. Then $8150 waits for it to be SET. So the game waits for the flag to clear, then waits for it to set — i.e., it waits for the START of the sprite-0 hit (falling edge then rising edge), precisely aligning with scanline ~31.\n\nHmm, but $0722 may be 0 normally (it's \"sprite 0 hit detection enabled\"?). If $0722==0, it skips the clear-wait and goes straight to $8150. Actually $0722 is likely the flag that enables timing. Let me read $0722. If it's nonzero, both waits run.\n\nActually, in SMB, $0722 is \"disable collision detection\" / it's set to 1 during normal play to enable the sprite-0-based scroll split. Let me read it.\n\nNow $0778 = 17 = 0x11. So bit0 = 1 (nametable 1), bit4 = 1 (sprite pattern table = $1000?). So $2000 = 0x11 → nametable select 1, sprite pattern $1000, NMI off (bit7=0). Hmm, but with ORA #$80 → 0x91 for NMI on.\n\nSo at $816C, $2000 = $11 (nametable 1) is written AFTER the scroll writes at the split — so the nametable switches from 0 (set at $808C) to 1 at the split. And the scroll 0,0 write at $80AB applies to nametable 0 (status bar). Great, fully consistent with the tool: status bar nametable 0, playfield nametable 1 (base select bit from $2000), and scroll_x from $073F.\n\nWait, but $816C happens after $815F/$8165. The order within scanline 31: scroll writes then $2000 write. Both take effect for the next scanline. Fine.\n\nSo the split is done by writing, in this order, at the sprite-0 hit (scanline ~31):\n1. $2005 = $073F (horizontal scroll = camera low byte)\n2. $2005 = $0740 (vertical scroll = 0)\n3. $2000 = $0778 (nametable select → 1, sprite pattern)\nThen later $817E re-enables NMI ($0778 | $80).\n\nAnd the status-bar scroll (0,0) + nametable 0 was set at $80AB/$80AC (via JSR $8EE6) and $808C ($2000 = $0778 & $7E = nametable 0).\n\nHmm, actually careful: the \"top fixed scroll\" is set at $80AB early in the NMI, and the nametable 0 at $808C. So the top of the frame (scanlines 0-31) uses scroll 0,0 and nametable 0 → the HUD. Then at the sprite-0 hit (scanline 31), the scroll becomes ($073F,$0740) and nametable 1 → the scrolling level. \n\nThat's the complete mechanism.\n\nNow the \"waiting code reads\": $2002 at $813D (wait for bit6 clear, if $0722) and at $8150 (wait for bit6 set). The loop at $8150 is the main wait.\n\nLet me read $0722 and confirm. Also, I should double check whether the $2005 write at scanline 31 truly uses $073F/$0740 and camera. Already confirmed: camera=257, $073F=1, and the tool reported playfield scroll_x=1.\n\nWait, camera=257 → low byte = 1 → scroll_x = 1 with nametable 1. Hmm, but the visible playfield with camera 257: absolute scroll 257 = nametable 1 base + 1. Yes.\n\nNow let me also verify the sprite 0: list_sprites shows OAM index 0: tile $FF, x=88, y=25, behind_background=true, palette 7. That's the HUD coin sprite. It's behind background but still generates a sprite-0 hit? Sprite 0 hit requires sprite pixel non-transparent over a non-transparent background pixel, and it doesn't matter if the sprite is behind background; it still hits. The sprite's opaque pixels are at rows 5-6 → y=30-31. That matches the split at scanline 31.\n\nGreat. So the answer:\n- Scanline: the status bar occupies scanlines 0–31 (top 4 tile rows, y 0–31). The scroll changes at scanline 31: after the sprite-0 hit at scanline 31, the game writes the new scroll at $815F/$8165; the playfield from scanline 32 down uses the camera scroll.\n- What it waits for: the sprite-0 hit — PPUSTATUS ($2002) bit 6 — set by the opaque pixels of sprite 0 (OAM #0, tile $FF, the HUD coin sprite at (88,25), rows 5-6 = y 30-31) overlapping the background coin tile. The polling loop is $8150-$8156 (`LDA $2002 / AND #$40 / BEQ $8150`), ~190 reads/frame; it exits when the read has $40 set (observed last read $5E).\n- Which code writes the new scroll: NMI at $815C-$8167: `LDA $073F / STA $2005 / LDA $0740 / STA $2005`, after a 20-iteration delay at $8157-$815A. Plus $8168-$816C writes $2000 = $0778 switching nametable. The status-bar scroll 0,0 is (re)written earlier in the same NMI at $80A9-$80AD via the helper $8EE6 (STA $2005 twice with A=0), and nametable 0 via $808C.\n\nNow, the task explicitly says \"trace_writes on $2005 (and on whatever the waiting code reads)\". Done: $2005 writers and $2002 readers.\n\nI should also mention the \"trace_writes on $2005\" from when the camera was 0 showed all zeros, and with camera nonzero showed value 1. Good evidence.\n\nNow, screenshots before and after scrolling: before = start view (camera 0), after = scrolled view (camera 257). Both show the HUD fixed. Good.\n\nHmm, one concern: the \"before\" screenshot was after reset+Start (camera 0). Actually I took it before any scrolling: sha 86ded..., MARIO 000000, WORLD 1-1, TIME 394, Mario on hill. That's the before. After: sha f0343..., MARIO 000100, WORLD 1-1, TIME 235, scrolled level. Both have HUD in the same place. Good.\n\nLet me also read $0722 to describe accurately whether the clear-wait ran. And maybe confirm the NMI entry at $8181 (RTS?). Let me read bytes 0x8181-0x8182.\n\nLet me do a final batch: read $0722 (1826), disassemble $8150 (already have via bytes), and maybe read $073F/$0740 once more, and take one more screenshot for the \"after\" (already have). \n\nActually I have enough. Let me read $0722 and get the bytes at 0x8181.\n\nAlso, I realize I should double check the exact scanline where the write happens relative to the tool: the tool says scroll_scanline 31. That is \"the scanline during which the scroll write occurred / took effect\". I'll report: split at scanline 31 (status bar = scanlines 0–31; playfield = 32–239).\n\nHmm, wait: Actually the tool'