{"type":"thinking","text":"$0722 = 1, so the clear-wait at $813D runs too (waits for bit6 to be clear before waiting for it to be set). Good — that's the \"wait for start of the sprite-0 hit\" edge detection.\n\nThe disassemble at 33121 gave garbage (off by one) — not needed.\n\nget_tile_at_pixel at (88,28): background tile $2E (the HUD coin icon) palette 3, and sprite OAM #0 tile $FF, behind_background true, opaque=false at that pixel (pixel_value 0). The opaque pixels are lower (rows 5-6, y30-31). Fine.\n\nNow I have everything. Let me record the mapping note and promote.\n\nBefore that, let me double-check my reading of $0722's role: at $8138 LDA $0722 / BEQ $815C. If $0722 != 0, it does the $2002 wait-for-clear at $813D-$8143, then $8144 checks pause. If $0722==0 it jumps to $815C (skipping the clear wait). Actually $813B BEQ $815C? bytes at $813B: F0 1F = BEQ +0x1F → next 0x813D + 0x1F = 0x815C. Yes.\n\nSo with $0722=1, the NMI: waits until sprite0 flag is clear ($813D), then pause check + two JSRs ($814A $8223, $814D $81C6), then $8150 waits until sprite0 flag set, delay, writes scroll.\n\nHmm, but $8144-$814F: LDA $0776 / LSR / BCS $814D (skip $8223 if paused); JSR $8223; $814D JSR $81C6. These run between the clear-wait and the set-wait. So the cycle: wait for flag clear (previous hit), do some work, wait for flag set (current frame's hit at scanline 31), then write scroll.\n\nWait, but the flag clears at the pre-render scanline (scanline -1), which is BEFORE the visible frame. And the clear-wait at $813D happens during vblank (NMI at scanline 241 of previous frame) — at that time the flag from the previous visible frame is set, so $813D waits for it to clear. When does it clear? At the pre-render scanline of the NEXT frame — i.e., ~20 scanlines later (scanline 261 = -1). So $813D waits through the rest of vblank. Then $814A/$814D work, then $8150 waits for the hit at scanline 31. So the NMI spans from scanline 241 through the next frame's scanline 31. Good.\n\nActually, one caveat: $2002 reads clear the flag too. So $813D waiting for \"clear\" would see clear immediately after its own read clears it? No — the loop at $813D reads $2002; if bit6 set, the read ALSO clears it, but the loop condition is checked on the value read (which had bit6 set), so it loops again; the next read now sees... the flag was cleared by the previous read, so bit6=0 → exits. Hmm! So this loop exits after two reads at most (the read clears the flag). Unless the sprite-0 hit continues to re-set the flag. Actually the sprite-0 flag, once set, stays set until read or pre-render; reading it clears it. So the loop `LDA $2002 / AND #$40 / BNE` would exit on the second iteration because the first read cleared the flag. That means $813D is not really \"waiting for the pre-render\", just consuming/clearing the flag. Hmm.\n\nWait, but the reads observed: PC $8140 had value 30 = 0x1E (bit6 clear) — 3 reads in 3 frames, i.e., 1 per frame, exiting immediately. So $813D read value 0x1E (clear) each frame → the flag was already cleared by the earlier $2002 read at $80A6 (LDX $2002). So $813D's BNE is false and it falls through. So it's not a wait loop here. Fine — it's a guard.\n\nSo the real wait is at $8150: waits for bit6 set. Reads there: value 0x1E (~190 times) then exits on 0x5E (bit6 set). Good.\n\nSo the wait is on the sprite-0 hit of the upcoming frame, ~190 polls/frame.\n\nNow I'm confident. Let me record the mapping.\n\nrecord_mapping: mapping_type \"note\", id \"scroll split\", name plain (e.g., \"Status-bar scroll split (sprite-0 timing)\"). Notes with scanline, method, code addresses.\n\nAlso I should note the $2005 writer addresses and the $2002 wait.\n\nLet me write the note text:\n\n\"Status-bar scroll split. The top 4 tile rows (screen scanlines 0-31) stay at scroll 0,0 nametable 0 while the playfield scrolls. Method: the NMI sets the fixed scroll 0,0 via the helper at $8EE6 (STA $2005 twice with A=0) at $80A9-$80AD, and PPUCTRL nametable 0